<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">National League for Democracy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1988">1988</a>–<a href="/years/2023">2023</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NLD</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The National League for Democracy is a political party of Myanmar founded in 1988 after the pro-democracy uprising, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi">Aung San Suu Kyi</a>. It won the 1990 election, which the military ignored, and the elections of 2015 and 2020. Its government was overthrown by the coup of February 2021, and the junta dissolved it in 2023.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/national-league-for-democracy.jpg" alt="National League for Democracy" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: National League for Democracy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_National_League_for_Democracy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The National League for Democracy was founded on 27 September 1988, in the aftermath of the 8888 Uprising, a series of pro-democracy protests that ended when the military again took control of the country. It formed under the leadership of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi">Aung San Suu Kyi</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a>, a leader of the Burmese independence movement of the 1940s, whom concerned democracy advocates had recruited.</p><p>Its first chair was Brigadier General Aung Gyi, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san-suu-kyi">Aung San Suu Kyi</a> was general secretary. The party advocated a non-violent movement towards multi-party democracy and said it supported human rights, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-of-law">rule of law</a> and national reconciliation. Its flag bore the peacock, and its emblem was a traditional bamboo hat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1990 elections the party took 59 per cent of the vote and won 392 of 492 contested seats, but the ruling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/military-junta">military junta</a> did not let it form a government. The party was repressed, and Suu Kyi was under house arrest for 16 of the 21 years after 1996. In May 2003 dozens of NLD members were shot and killed in a military-sponsored massacre at Depayin.</p><p>The NLD boycotted the 2010 election because many prominent members were barred from standing, and was declared illegal on 6 May 2010. It re-registered in December 2011, won 43 of 45 seats in the 2012 by-elections and won a supermajority in the 2015 general election, which made possible the country's first non-military president in 54 years. Critics faulted it for its response to military violence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohingya">Rohingya</a> from 2016.</p><p>The NLD won the 2020 election by a larger margin. The military claimed 8.6 million irregularities in voter lists, which the Union Election Commission rejected on 28 January 2021, and on 1 February the military seized power and detained Suu Kyi, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/win-myint">Win Myint</a> and other leaders. Hundreds of NLD members were arrested. In January 2023 the junta enacted an electoral law designed to favour the military's proxy, the Union Solidarity and Development Party; the NLD refused to re-register, and the commission dissolved it on 28 March 2023, a decision the party rejected as illegitimate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The party was described as liberal, social-liberal and, historically, social-democratic, and also as having no rigid ideology. It had a Women's Committee, which provided legal and social assistance to women in need, with committees at region, state, ward and village level. Suu Kyi called the 2008 constitution, drafted with the input of the armed forces and reserving 25 per cent of parliamentary seats for military appointees, undemocratic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “National League for Democracy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…formed the State Law and Order Restoration Council (SLORC), renamed in 1997 the State Peace and Development Council, which ignored the election of 1990 won by Aung San Suu Kyi's <b>National League for Democracy</b> (NLD) and held her under house arrest for much of the next twenty years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
